<main>

<h1>PyrusLLM</h1>
<p class="lede">An LLM inference node distributed over Pear. It installs over P2P,
updates itself over OTA, and exposes your machine to the network as an
OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</p>

<div class="pills">
  <span class="pill">v0.12.1</span>
  <span class="pill">Bare runtime</span>
  <span class="pill">OpenAI-compatible</span>
  <span class="pill">Ed25519 signed manifest</span>
  <span class="pill">x402 payments</span>
</div>

<h2 id="overview">Overview <a class="anch" href="#overview">#</a></h2>
<p>PyrusLLM turns a machine into a node on a peer-to-peer inference marketplace.
Each node announces a <strong>signed manifest</strong> declaring which model it serves, what it
charges and how loaded it is. Any node can ask any other for a completion, and the
answer arrives with provenance attached: which machine produced it, how fast, and
what it cost.</p>

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="ico">🖥️</div><b>Runs locally</b>
    <p>Weights travel over hypercore, not HTTP, and are cached on disk. Nothing
    leaves your machine unless you ask it to.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="ico">🔗</div><b>Joins a network</b>
    <p>A fixed DHT topic discovers peers. Only nodes whose signed manifest
    verifies become routing candidates.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="ico">💸</div><b>Gets paid</b>
    <p>The manifest carries a payout address. Paid requests settle over x402 and
    come back with a receipt and an attestation.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="cli-or-api">Is PyrusLLM a CLI or an API? <a class="anch" href="#cli-or-api">#</a></h2>
<p><strong>It is both</strong>, and the distinction matters because they are used by
different people at different moments.</p>

<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>What it is</th><th>Who uses it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><code>pyrusllm &lt;cmd&gt;</code></td>
  <td>A <strong>CLI</strong> you run in a terminal. It is how you start the node, join the
      network, run a one-off prompt, move files and manage the wallet.</td>
  <td>The <strong>operator</strong> of the machine.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>http://localhost:8787/v1/…</code></td>
  <td>An <strong>HTTP API</strong> served by <code>pyrusllm serve</code>. The
      <code>/v1/chat/completions</code> and <code>/v1/models</code> routes are
      OpenAI-compatible; the rest are PyrusLLM's own.</td>
  <td><strong>Clients and agents</strong> — any OpenAI SDK, curl, Open WebUI, a Telegram bot.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<div class="note i">
<b>The relationship:</b> the CLI command <code>pyrusllm serve</code> is what
<em>starts</em> the API. Everything the browser panels do, they do through that same
public API with a Bearer key — there is no privileged path reserved for the UI.
</div>

<h2 id="install">Install <a class="anch" href="#install">#</a></h2>
<h4>Over npm</h4>
<pre><code>npm install -g pyrusllm</code></pre>
<h4>Over P2P (Pear)</h4>
<pre><code>pear install pear://8f789f6hsf4ghymku5eqwqmbqiubiigp8xpy6boiymunnbyznpny</code></pre>
<div class="note i">The npm package ships a Node launcher that delegates to the
<strong>Bare</strong> runtime; the node itself does not run on Node. The correct Bare binary for
your platform is pulled in as an optional dependency at install time.</div>

<h2 id="quickstart">Quickstart <a class="anch" href="#quickstart">#</a></h2>
<h4>1. Answer a prompt, fully offline</h4>
<pre><code>pyrusllm prompt <span class="c-st">"Explain peer-to-peer in one sentence"</span></code></pre>

<h4>2. Start the gateway and join the network</h4>
<pre><code>pyrusllm serve <span class="c-fl">--swarm</span> <span class="c-fl">--operator</span> <span class="c-st">"your name"</span></code></pre>

<h4>3. Call it like OpenAI</h4>
<pre><code>curl http://localhost:8787/v1/chat/completions \
  -H <span class="c-st">"Authorization: Bearer $PYRUS_KEY"</span> \
  -H <span class="c-st">"Content-Type: application/json"</span> \
  -d <span class="c-st">'{"model":"llama1b","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}],"stream":true}'</span></code></pre>

<div class="note w">You need a key for step 3. Create one from the
<a href="#panels">My Node panel</a> or with <a href="#ep-keys-new">POST /v1/keys</a>.</div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="cli">CLI — global flags <a class="anch" href="#cli">#</a></h2>
<p>Run <code>pyrusllm --help</code> to list every command, or
<code>pyrusllm &lt;command&gt; --help</code> for one command's full detail.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm [flags] [command]</code></pre>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>--version</code>, <code>-v</code></td><td>Print the current version</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--storage &lt;dir&gt;</code></td><td>Custom storage directory</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--no-updates</code></td><td>Disable OTA updates for this run</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--port &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>App HTTP port (default 8787)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--no-serve</code></td><td>Only the OTA updater, without starting the app</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--no-open</code></td><td>Do not open the browser on start</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--update-delay &lt;ms&gt;</code></td><td>OTA jitter window in ms (default 10000)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note w"><b>One process per storage directory.</b> The Corestore takes a
lock on it. A second node against the same <code>--storage</code> fails with
"File descriptor could not be locked" — use a different directory, or
<code>--no-store</code>.</div>

<h2 id="cli-prompt"><code>pyrusllm prompt</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-prompt">#</a></h2>
<p>Answer a prompt with 100% local inference. Loads an LLM with QVAC and answers
without leaving this machine.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm prompt <span class="c-st">"&lt;prompt&gt;"</span> [flags]</code></pre>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Argument / flag</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>&lt;prompt&gt;</code></td><td>The text to answer, or <code>-</code> to read it from stdin</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--model &lt;alias&gt;</code></td><td>Model alias or exact registry name (default <code>llama1b</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--ctx &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>Context size</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--gpu-layers &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>Layers to send to the GPU. <code>0</code> = all CPU</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--no-download</code></td><td>Fail instead of downloading the weights when not cached</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--quiet</code>, <code>-q</code></td><td>Print only the answer, no diagnostics or measurements</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note i"><b>Use <code>-</code> on Windows for non-ASCII prompts.</b> The standalone
binary receives argv in the ANSI codepage, which mangles accented characters.
stdin is a byte stream and does not go through that conversion:
<code>echo "Respondé…" | pyrusllm prompt -</code></div>
<div class="note w">On a weak iGPU (Intel UHD 620), <code>--gpu-layers 0</code> measured
<strong>5× faster</strong> than letting the SDK decide. See <code>NOTES.md</code>.</div>

<h2 id="cli-serve"><code>pyrusllm serve</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-serve">#</a></h2>
<p>Start the OpenAI-compatible gateway and the browser panels. <strong>This is the command
that turns on the HTTP API.</strong></p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm serve [flags]</code></pre>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>--port &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>Gateway HTTP port (default 8787)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--swarm</code></td><td>Join the P2P topic and populate the registry with verified peers</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--operator &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Operator name announced in the manifest</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--demo</code></td><td>Populate the registry with simulated nodes (1 real + 3 mocks)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--no-store</code></td><td>Do not open the Hyperbee/Hyperdrive: no persistence, no files</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--gpu-layers &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>Layers to send to the real node's GPU. <code>0</code> = all CPU</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note i">Without <code>--swarm</code> the gateway serves the panels and the API but
stays off the network: the registry starts <strong>empty</strong> and requests for a model
nobody serves return a clear "no nodes serving that model" error. That is the honest
state, not a bug.</div>

<h2 id="cli-peers"><code>pyrusllm peers</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-peers">#</a></h2>
<p>Join the P2P topic and list the peers with a verified manifest, without starting
the gateway. Reports join → first peer and join → first verified manifest timings.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm peers [flags]</code></pre>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>--operator &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Operator name announced in the manifest</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--timeout &lt;s&gt;</code></td><td>Exit after N seconds (default: never exits, Ctrl+C)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--expect &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>Exit code 1 if fewer than N verified peers on exit</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note w"><b>Discovery takes ~17 s.</b> Run this on both machines with
<strong>overlapping windows</strong> — a short <code>--timeout</code> on one side while the other
has not joined yet reports 0 peers and looks like a network fault when it is not.</div>

<h2 id="cli-send"><code>pyrusllm send</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-send">#</a></h2>
<p>Publish a file or folder into this node's Hyperdrive and announce it on the DHT.
Prints a <code>qvac://</code> link.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm send &lt;path&gt; [<span class="c-fl">--as</span> &lt;name&gt;]</code></pre>
<div class="note w"><b>The process stays running on purpose.</b> Hypercore is not
store-and-forward: there is no server holding the file. The bytes come out of your
machine, so it must stay on while the other side downloads.</div>

<h2 id="cli-fetch"><code>pyrusllm fetch</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-fetch">#</a></h2>
<p>Download a file published by another machine.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm fetch qvac://&lt;key&gt;/&lt;path&gt; [<span class="c-fl">--out</span> &lt;dir&gt;] [<span class="c-fl">--timeout</span> &lt;s&gt;]</code></pre>
<div class="note g">Every block is verified against the drive's merkle root as it
arrives, so a file tampered with midway cannot complete. What the key does
<strong>not</strong> prove is <em>whose</em> it is — that depends on the channel the link
arrived through.</div>

<h2 id="cli-files"><code>pyrusllm files</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-files">#</a></h2>
<p>List the files published by this node, or by a peer.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm files [<span class="c-fl">--link</span> qvac://…] [<span class="c-fl">--timeout</span> &lt;s&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>With <code>--link</code> it lists a remote drive without downloading the content:
Hyperdrive metadata replicates separately from the blobs.</p>

<h2 id="cli-wallet"><code>pyrusllm wallet</code> <a class="anch" href="#cli-wallet">#</a></h2>
<p>Show, create or restore this node's payout wallet.</p>
<pre><code>pyrusllm wallet [<span class="c-fl">--create</span> | <span class="c-fl">--restore</span> <span class="c-st">"&lt;24 words&gt;"</span>]</code></pre>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><em>(none)</em></td><td>Show the payout address, or say there is no wallet yet</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--create</code></td><td>Generate a new wallet for this node</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--restore &lt;phrase&gt;</code></td><td>Restore from the 24 words of a backup</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note b"><b>The 24 words are shown once.</b> Write them down. Without them
<em>and</em> without the keystore, the wallet is lost. The seed is stored encrypted with
<code>PYRUS_WALLET_PASSPHRASE</code>; if that <code>.env</code> lives next to the keystore, the
encryption protects against a backup or a repo — not against somebody who already
has access to the machine.</div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="auth">Authentication <a class="anch" href="#auth">#</a></h2>
<p>Every API route except the three noted below requires a Bearer key:</p>
<pre><code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Keys are created per client so a single bot can be cut off without touching the
rest. They are stored on the machine and <strong>survive a restart</strong> — they have to: the
spend cap is counted per key, and a registry that reset with the process would be a
cap you could clear by restarting the node.</p>

<h4>Routes that need no key</h4>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>GET /v1/models</code></td><td>An OpenAI client must discover the catalog before it has a key. The data is sanitised instead — no operator, no pricing.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GET /v1/receipts/:id</code></td><td>Whoever paid through a 402 has no key by definition. Demanding one would leave them unable to audit what they paid for.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GET /v1/keys/panel</code></td><td>The bootstrap the browser panel uses to obtain its own key.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="errors">Errors <a class="anch" href="#errors">#</a></h2>
<p>Errors come back in OpenAI shape:</p>
<pre><code>{ <span class="c-kw">"error"</span>: { <span class="c-kw">"message"</span>: <span class="c-st">"…"</span>, <span class="c-kw">"type"</span>: <span class="c-st">"…"</span> } }</code></pre>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>400</code></td><td>Malformed body or invalid parameters</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Missing, unknown or revoked key</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>402</code></td><td>Payment required — see <a href="#x402">the x402 flow</a>. Also returned when the spend cap is hit, <em>without</em> an <code>accepts</code> array.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>No node serving that model, or unknown receipt id</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>502</code></td><td>The chosen provider failed mid-request</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-chat">Create a chat completion <a class="anch" href="#ep-chat">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/chat/completions</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>OpenAI-compatible. The gateway picks a node by load and price, streams the answer
back, and attaches provenance headers.</p>

<h4>Body</h4>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>string</td><td>Model id or alias, e.g. <code>llama1b</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>messages</code></td><td>array</td><td>Standard <code>{role, content}</code> turns</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>stream</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>SSE streaming when <code>true</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>local</code></td><td>boolean</td><td><strong>PyrusLLM extension.</strong> Keep the request on this machine; never route to a peer or an external API</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>node</code></td><td>string</td><td><strong>PyrusLLM extension.</strong> Pin one specific machine by id. No fallback if it is busy</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h4>Response headers</h4>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>X-Pyrus-Operator</code></td><td>Who answered (URI-encoded)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>X-Pyrus-Kind</code></td><td><code>real</code> · <code>peer</code> · <code>mock</code> · <code>upstream</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>X-Pyrus-Scope</code></td><td><code>local</code> or <code>external</code> — whether the prompt left the network</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>X-Pyrus-Model</code></td><td>The model that actually served it</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>X-Pyrus-Cost-Estimate-Micros</code></td><td>The <strong>ceiling</strong> the spend was authorised against, in micro-dollars. Not the final cost: with SSE the headers go out before the first token.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<div class="note i"><b><code>X-Pyrus-Scope</code> is the field that matters for privacy.</b>
<code>kind: upstream</code> does not by itself mean the prompt left your machine — a
llama-server on localhost is also an upstream. <code>scope: external</code> is the one that
says a third party saw it.</div>

<h4>Example</h4>
<pre><code>curl http://localhost:8787/v1/chat/completions \
  -H <span class="c-st">"Authorization: Bearer $PYRUS_KEY"</span> \
  -H <span class="c-st">"Content-Type: application/json"</span> \
  -d <span class="c-st">'{
        "model": "llama1b",
        "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"hello"}],
        "stream": true,
        "local": false
      }'</span></code></pre>

<div class="note i">When streaming, a <strong>final SSE event</strong> may carry
<code>paymentResponse</code> and <code>attestation</code> instead of a completion chunk — it has
no <code>choices</code>. See <a href="#x402">the x402 flow</a>.</div>

<h2 id="ep-models">List models <a class="anch" href="#ep-models">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/models</span><span class="auth open">No key</span></div>
<p>OpenAI-compatible catalog of every model the network announces.</p>
<pre><code>{
  <span class="c-kw">"object"</span>: <span class="c-st">"list"</span>,
  <span class="c-kw">"data"</span>: [{ <span class="c-kw">"id"</span>: <span class="c-st">"llama1b"</span>, <span class="c-kw">"object"</span>: <span class="c-st">"model"</span>, <span class="c-kw">"owned_by"</span>: <span class="c-st">"pyrusllm"</span> }]
}</code></pre>
<div class="note i">Deliberately sanitised: it names the model, never the operator or
the price. Those live behind <a href="#ep-nodes">/v1/nodes</a>, which needs a key.</div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-nodes">List nodes <a class="anch" href="#ep-nodes">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/nodes</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>The rich marketplace view: operator, price, load, kind. Returns
<code>{ nodes, swarm }</code>; <code>swarm</code> is <code>null</code> when this gateway has not joined
the P2P network yet.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Node kind</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>real</code></td><td>This machine</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>peer</code></td><td>A genuinely remote node, discovered over the swarm, signed manifest verified</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mock</code></td><td>Simulated (<code>--demo</code>). Always labelled as such in the UI</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>upstream</code></td><td>Reached over HTTP. May be a third-party API <em>or</em> a local engine — check <code>local</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>known</code></td><td>In the directory, manifest verified once, but no socket now. Never a routing candidate</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="ep-agent">Agent status <a class="anch" href="#ep-agent">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/agent</span><span class="auth open">No key</span></div>
<p>Whether this machine has joined the network. Statuses:
<code>offline</code>, <code>launching</code>, <code>live</code>, <code>error</code>.
Also carries <code>canLaunch</code> — false when the gateway was started without swarm support.</p>

<h2 id="ep-launch">Launch the agent <a class="anch" href="#ep-launch">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/agent/launch</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Joins the P2P network at runtime — the API equivalent of restarting with
<code>--swarm</code>. Requires a gateway started with launch support.</p>

<h2 id="ep-manifest">Swarm manifest <a class="anch" href="#ep-manifest">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/swarm/manifest</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/swarm/manifest</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p><code>GET</code> returns the signed manifest, the model catalog with a
<code>fits</code> flag per model (does it fit in this machine's RAM), and
<code>modelLoad</code> status. <code>POST</code> edits <code>displayName</code>, <code>tags</code>,
<code>maxConcurrentRequests</code> or <code>modelId</code> and <strong>re-signs</strong>.</p>
<div class="note w">Switching <code>modelId</code> triggers a real model load. It can take
several seconds or fail for lack of memory, while the node keeps answering with the
current model. On failure the current model is kept.</div>

<h2 id="ep-log">Routing log <a class="anch" href="#ep-log">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/routing-log</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Both directions of the exchange, separated by <code>kind</code>: <code>served</code> is what
this node produced for a peer, <code>route</code> is what it asked of somebody else. Each
entry carries tokens, TTFT, tok/s, the D25 prefill/decode split with its
<code>tokensFuente</code> provenance, and <code>finishReason</code>.</p>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-keys">List keys <a class="anch" href="#ep-keys">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/keys</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Returns <code>{ keys: [{ id, label, key, lastUsedAt }] }</code>.</p>

<h2 id="ep-keys-new">Create a key <a class="anch" href="#ep-keys-new">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/keys</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<pre><code>curl -X POST http://localhost:8787/v1/keys \
  -H <span class="c-st">"Authorization: Bearer $PYRUS_KEY"</span> \
  -H <span class="c-st">"Content-Type: application/json"</span> \
  -d <span class="c-st">'{"label":"telegram bot"}'</span></code></pre>

<h2 id="ep-keys-del">Revoke keys <a class="anch" href="#ep-keys-del">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m del">DELETE</span><span class="p">/v1/keys/:id</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/keys/revoke-all</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Revocation takes effect immediately — whatever is using the key stops working.</p>
<div class="note w"><code>revoke-all</code> kills the browser panel's own key too. The panel
re-credentials itself automatically; a script would need to fetch a new key.</div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-quota">Free-tier quota <a class="anch" href="#ep-quota">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/quota</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>What this node <strong>gives away</strong>: <code>given_tokens</code>, <code>quota_tokens</code>
per peer, <code>window_hours</code> (a sliding window, so it tops back up), and per-peer
usage. Enforced here, by the machine paying the electricity — not by whoever is asking.</p>

<h2 id="ep-budget">Spend cap <a class="anch" href="#ep-budget">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/budget</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/budget/report</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>What this node <strong>spends</strong> on external APIs. Local inference is in neither meter —
it costs nobody anything but your electricity.</p>
<div class="note i"><b>There are two caps</b> and either can be the one that cuts: a
per-client cap and a per-machine total. Show the <strong>smaller</strong> remainder — with the
account at USD 20 and the node at USD 2, saying "you have 20 left" promises nineteen
that do not exist.</div>

<h2 id="ep-receipts">Look up a receipt <a class="anch" href="#ep-receipts">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/receipts/:id</span><span class="auth open">No key</span></div>
<p>Takes the completion id (<code>chatcmpl-…</code>) that came back with the answer, and
returns the settlement receipt plus the attestation.</p>
<div class="note w">Receipts live in <strong>process memory</strong> and only the last 200 are
kept. This is not a ledger — the real ledger is the chain. A restart wipes them.
There is deliberately no route that <em>lists</em> receipts.</div>

<h2 id="x402">The x402 payment flow <a class="anch" href="#x402">#</a></h2>
<p>A request to a node with a wallet, without a valid credential, gets a
<code>402</code> whose body is not an error string but a <strong>quote</strong>: how much, to whom, on
which network, and up to how many tokens.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c-cm">// 402 response</span>
{ <span class="c-kw">"accepts"</span>: [{ <span class="c-kw">"scheme"</span>: …, <span class="c-kw">"network"</span>: …, <span class="c-kw">"payTo"</span>: …, <span class="c-kw">"maxAmountRequired"</span>: … }] }</code></pre>
<p>After paying, the answer streams back and a <strong>final SSE event</strong> carries the proof:</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it proves</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>paymentResponse</code></td><td>Somebody paid — the settlement receipt</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>attestation</code></td><td>What this node served, signed with the payout wallet over the JCS form of everything except the signature</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>attestationMissing</code></td><td>Present instead, with the reason, when no attestation could be produced</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note g"><b>Verify it yourself.</b> The attestation's <code>outputHash</code> is
over the <strong>text</strong>, not the chunking — so a provider that inflates the gateway's chunk
count by chopping the stream finer cannot move it. Recompute it against the answer you
actually received.</div>
<div class="note b">A <code>402</code> <em>without</em> an <code>accepts</code> array is a different
thing entirely: your spend cap is exhausted. There is nothing to pay — a limit was hit.</div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-wallet">Wallet status <a class="anch" href="#ep-wallet">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/wallet</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>The payout address, the chains and the settlement asset. Returns
<code>configured: false</code> when there is no wallet — a normal state for a node that only
consumes, not an error.</p>

<h2 id="ep-balances">Balances <a class="anch" href="#ep-balances">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/wallet/balances</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/wallet/history</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Native and token balances, and the movement history read from the explorer.</p>

<h2 id="ep-send">Send funds <a class="anch" href="#ep-send">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/wallet/send/quote</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/wallet/send</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Two steps on purpose: <code>quote</code> estimates gas and returns the exact fields, and
<code>send</code> signs and broadcasts <em>those same fields</em>. Re-reading the form between the
two would open the door to reviewing one thing and sending another.</p>
<div class="note b"><b>Mainnet requires explicit confirmation.</b> Send
<code>confirmar: "MAINNET"</code> in the body. This moves real money and cannot be undone.
<strong>The node signs, never the browser</strong> — the seed never leaves the process that opens it.</div>

<h2 id="ep-wallet-rest">Other wallet routes <a class="anch" href="#ep-wallet-rest">#</a></h2>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>POST /v1/wallet/create</code></td><td>Create a wallet (empty body) or import one (<code>{ frase }</code>). Returns the 24 words <strong>once</strong> for a new wallet</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>POST /v1/wallet/network</code></td><td>Switch network. No hot-swap: it saves and asks for a restart. Mainnet needs <code>confirmar: "MAINNET"</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>POST /v1/wallet/tokens</code></td><td>Watch an ERC-20: <code>{ address, symbol, decimals }</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DELETE /v1/wallet/tokens</code></td><td>Stop watching a token</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-files">List files <a class="anch" href="#ep-files">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/files</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Lists this node's Hyperdrive. Pass <code>?peerKey=&lt;hex&gt;</code> to list a peer's drive
instead — without it you always get the local one, whichever node you meant.</p>

<h2 id="ep-upload">Upload and fetch <a class="anch" href="#ep-upload">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/files/upload?name=&lt;name&gt;</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/files/fetch</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Upload sends the raw body as the file content. Fetch downloads a
<code>qvac://</code> link to this node's disk — the API equivalent of
<a href="#cli-fetch"><code>pyrusllm fetch</code></a>.</p>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="ep-upstream">External assistant <a class="anch" href="#ep-upstream">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m get">GET</span><span class="p">/v1/upstream</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<p>Lists the configured upstreams and whether each has its credential. Configuring a
new upstream or changing a credential is <strong>not</strong> possible over HTTP — that lives in
<code>upstreams.json</code> on the operator's disk.</p>

<h2 id="ep-optin">Opt in or out <a class="anch" href="#ep-optin">#</a></h2>
<div class="ep"><span class="v m post">POST</span><span class="p">/v1/upstream/opt-in</span><span class="auth">Bearer</span></div>
<pre><code>{ <span class="c-kw">"enabled"</span>: <span class="c-kw">true</span> }</code></pre>
<div class="note b"><b>This is the one path where a prompt leaves the P2P network</b> and
goes to a company's API, which sees it, may log it, and bills it. <strong>Off by default.</strong>
Even switched on it only competes when nobody local or on the network has capacity, and
never when the request asks to stay on this machine.</div>
<div class="note i">Turning it on here does not write the config file: on restart it
goes back to whatever <code>upstreams.json</code> says.</div>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<h2 id="panels">Browser panels <a class="anch" href="#panels">#</a></h2>
<p><code>pyrusllm serve</code> also serves four pages. They are plain clients of the API
above — same Bearer key, no privileged path.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Panel</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>/</code></td><td><strong>Chat</strong> — ask the network; every answer names the machine that produced it</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/node</code></td><td><strong>My Node</strong> — your machine as a provider: status, load, rate, keys, traffic, meters</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/network</code></td><td><strong>Network</strong> — every node this machine knows about, and their files</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/wallet</code></td><td><strong>Wallet</strong> — address, balance, movements, send, network and token settings</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/admin</code></td><td><strong>Admin</strong> — all nodes plus the raw routing log</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="env">Environment <a class="anch" href="#env">#</a></h2>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>PYRUS_WALLET_PASSPHRASE</code></td><td>Encrypts the wallet seed. Required by <code>wallet --create</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PYRUS_WALLET_RED</code></td><td>Pins the network. When set, the panel's network selector is disabled</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PYRUS_WALLET_RPC</code></td><td>Override the RPC endpoint</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PYRUS_X402_FACILITATOR</code></td><td>x402 facilitator URL</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PYRUS_X402_PLASMA_ASSET_VERIFICADO</code></td><td>Must be <code>1</code> to charge on Plasma mainnet — a human step: verify the USD₮0 contract on the explorer first</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>All of these can live in a <code>.env</code> in the storage directory.</p>

<h2 id="models">Models <a class="anch" href="#models">#</a></h2>
<p>Aliases accepted by <code>--model</code> and by the <code>model</code> field:</p>
<div class="pills">
  <span class="pill">smol</span>
  <span class="pill">llama1b <em>(default)</em></span>
  <span class="pill">qwen1_7b</span>
  <span class="pill">qwen4b</span>
  <span class="pill">qwen8b</span>
  <span class="pill">gemma4b</span>
</div>
<p style="margin-top:.9rem">An exact registry name also works. The catalog exposed by
<a href="#ep-manifest">/v1/swarm/manifest</a> marks each model with <code>fits</code>, computed
against this machine's RAM.</p>

<div class="foot">
  PyrusLLM v0.12.1 · Apache-2.0 · Generated from the source of
  <code>bin.mjs</code> and <code>qvac/gateway.mjs</code>.
</div>

</main>
</div>

<script>
(function () {
  // Theme toggle. Starts on the viewer's system preference and only pins an
  // explicit choice, so the page keeps following the OS until someone asks.
  var KEY = 'pyrusdocs.theme'
  try {
    var saved = localStorage.getItem(KEY)
    if (saved) document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', saved)
  } catch (e) {}
  function flip () {
    var cur = document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-theme')
    if (!cur) {
      cur = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light'
    }
    var next = cur === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark'
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', next)
    try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, next) } catch (e) {}
  }
  var t2 = document.getElementById('theme2')
  if (t2) t2.addEventListener('click', flip)

  // Mobile drawer.
  var side = document.getElementById('side')
  var scrim = document.getElementById('scrim')
  var menu = document.getElementById('menu')
  function close () { side.classList.remove('open'); scrim.classList.remove('on') }
  if (menu) {
    menu.addEventListener('click', function () {
      side.classList.toggle('open')
      scrim.classList.toggle('on')
    })
  }
  scrim.addEventListener('click', close)

  // Active-section highlighting. IntersectionObserver over every anchored
  // heading; the last one to cross the top band wins.
  var links = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('#nav a'))
  var byHash = {}
  links.forEach(function (a) { byHash[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)] = a })
  links.forEach(function (a) { a.addEventListener('click', close) })

  var heads = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('main h2[id]'))
  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window && heads.length) {
    var seen = {}
    var obs = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
      entries.forEach(function (en) { seen[en.target.id] = en.isIntersecting })
      var active = null
      for (var i = 0; i < heads.length; i++) {
        if (seen[heads[i].id]) { active = heads[i].id; break }
      }
      if (!active) return
      links.forEach(function (a) { a.classList.remove('on') })
      if (byHash[active]) byHash[active].classList.add('on')
    }, { rootMargin: '0px 0px -78% 0px' })
    heads.forEach(function (h) { obs.observe(h) })
  }
})()
</script>
</body>
</html>
